    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Roof replacements in Utah typically cost between $20,000 and $30,000 for an average home. Several main financing paths are available to help manage these costs over time.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Several main financing paths are available to help manage these costs over time"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Common Financing Options in Utah"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Contractor & Point-of-Sale Financing: Many Utah roofing companies partner with lenders (such as Momnt Technologies via Contractor Loan PRO, Goodleap, or regional financial institutions) to offer fast pre-qualification with a soft credit check.",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Terms: $0 down options, promotional 0% interest or 0-payment periods (often 12 months), or fixed repayment terms ranging from 10 to 20 years (up to 120+ months)."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Examples: Local companies like Legacy Roofing , Remi Roofing , Big Horn Roofing , and Rooval Roofing provide in-house or partner-backed payment plans.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  0,
                  2,
                  3,
                  4,
                  5,
                  6,
                  7
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2,
              3,
              4,
              5,
              6,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Home Equity Lines of Credit (HELOCs) or Home Equity Loans: If you have built up equity in your Utah home, a HELOC or second mortgage lets you borrow against it, often with lower interest rates than unsecured personal loans."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Unsecured Personal Loans: Independent bank or credit union personal loans offer quick funding ($1,000 to $100,000) with fixed terms (typically 2 to 7 years), requiring no home equity collateral.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Government-Backed & Rural Programs:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "USDA Rural Development Section 504 Repair Loans and Grants : For very-low-income rural Utah homeowners, Section 504 offers up to a $40,000 loan at a 1% fixed interest rate (20-year term) and up to $10,000 in grants for qualifying health and safety hazards (grants are restricted to homeowners aged 62 or older).",
                "snippet_links": [
                  {
                    "text": "USDA Rural Development Section 504 Repair Loans and Grants",
                    "link": "https://www.rd.usda.gov/programs-services/single-family-housing-programs/single-family-housing-repair-loans-grants/ut"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "snippet": "FHA 203(k) or Title I Loans: Government-insured rehabilitation and home improvement loans that can be rolled into a mortgage or used as standalone property improvement financing.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  1,
                  8,
                  9
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              8,
              9
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Tips Before You Sign"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Check for Soft Credit Checks: Ask contractors if their pre-qualification tool uses a soft pull so it won't drop your credit score.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Watch Deferred Interest Traps: If you choose a 0% promotional period (e.g., 12 months no interest), verify whether deferred interest applies retroactively if the balance isn't paid off before the window ends.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Ask About Insurance & Deductibles: Some Utah contractors assist with storm claim paperwork or coordinate deductible payment options if your replacement is tied to hail or wind damage.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              10
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
